{"schemaVersion":1,"key":"fundamentals/training","track":"engineers","lang":"en","dir":"ltr","title":"Training LLMs","objectives":{"lang":"en","items":["Explain what training changes inside a model, in terms of its parameters","Distinguish pre-training from fine-tuning, by the data each one needs","Explain when fine-tuning is worth its cost, and when it is not","Describe what PEFT changes, and why it is the common choice","Name the tools you would actually reach for: Unsloth and Hugging Face"]},"translatedObjectives":null,"prerequisites":[{"key":"fundamentals/llms","number":1,"title":"LLM Fundamentals","drawn":true,"track":"engineers","lang":"en"}],"previous":{"key":"fundamentals/llms","number":1,"title":"LLM Fundamentals","drawn":true,"track":"engineers","lang":"en"},"next":{"key":"fundamentals/rag","number":3,"title":"RAG & Embeddings","drawn":true,"track":"engineers","lang":"en"},"toc":[{"id":"what-training-means","text":"What Training Means?","depth":2},{"id":"pre-training","text":"Pre-training","depth":2},{"id":"why-we-dont-have-to-pre-train","text":"Why We don't Have to Pre-train?","depth":2},{"id":"fine-tuning","text":"Fine-tuning","depth":2},{"id":"does-it-actually-work","text":"Does it Actually Work?","depth":3},{"id":"parameter-efficient-fine-tuning-peft","text":"Parameter-efficient Fine-tuning (PEFT)","depth":2},{"id":"unsloth","text":"Unsloth","depth":2},{"id":"comparing-side-by-side","text":"Comparing Side-by-side","depth":2},{"id":"when-not-to-fine-tune","text":"When Not to Fine-tune","depth":2},{"id":"what-is-hugging-face","text":"What is Hugging Face?","depth":2},{"id":"key-terms","text":"Key Terms","depth":2},{"id":"summary","text":"Summary","depth":2},{"id":"references","text":"References","depth":2},{"id":"-articles","text":"📄 Articles","depth":3},{"id":"-videos","text":"🎬 Videos","depth":3},{"id":"️-tools","text":"🛠️ Tools","depth":3}],"body":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"module","key":"fundamentals/llms","track":"engineers","lang":"en"},"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"LLM Fundamentals"}]},{"type":"text","text":" said that an LLM is a network with a huge number of parameters. It also said that an LLM predicts the next word. This module is about where those parameters come from."}],"lead":true},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"There are two steps, and they are almost completely different. One step costs millions of dollars, and only a few labs can do it. You can run the other step tonight on your own GPU."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"what-training-means","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"What Training Means?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"At the start, a model knows nothing. Every parameter is a random number, so its output is random too."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Training means that you give the model text and change those numbers by a small amount. You do this again and again, until its guesses stop being random and start being right."}]},{"type":"image","number":"2.1","title":"An untrained network next to a trained one","note":"The same network before and after training. The input is \"The students opened their\". The random weights on the left produce \"Sun\". The learned weights on the right produce \"Laptops\". The shape of the network did not change. Only the numbers inside it changed.","src":"course-images/fundamentals/training-network.webp","alt":"An untrained network next to a trained one","width":1024,"height":573},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"That small change, repeated many times, is all that training does. Training does not add rules or facts in a form that you can open and read. It only adjusts numbers. The knowledge ends up spread across billions of them. This is why people still use Andrej Karpathy's description: you can think of training as "},{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"compressing the internet"}]},{"type":"text","text":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"pre-training","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Pre-training"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"The first step is "},{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"pre-training"}]},{"type":"text","text":", and it is by far the largest."}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"The model starts with parameters (its \"weights\") that are random and untrained."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"It reads a very large part of the internet: books, websites, code and articles. From this text, it learns to predict the next word. It does this again and again, billions of times."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Pre-training updates "},{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"all"}]},{"type":"text","text":" of the model's parameters. For a large model, that means billions of numbers change."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"It needs thousands of GPUs that run for weeks or months. This is why pre-training a large model from scratch costs "},{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"millions of dollars"}]},{"type":"text","text":". Only a small number of big labs (OpenAI, Google, Anthropic, Meta and a few others) can afford it."}]}]}]},{"type":"diagram","number":"2.2","title":"Pre-training","svg":{"light":"api/v1/diagrams/9e3a02218c71089ae951f70dd45e7d48.svg","dark":"api/v1/diagrams/5989245a9b557c3f3aebd2036f95c3b7.svg"}},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"The result is a "},{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"general-purpose"}]},{"type":"text","text":" model: good at many things, specialised in nothing."}]},{"type":"quote","blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"NOTE, and this one is an advanced topic."}]},{"type":"text","text":" Notice that nobody labelled that internet text. There is no answer key. The model makes its own training signal. It hides the next word, makes a guess, and checks the guess against the word that was really there. So the data labels itself. This is called "},{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"self-supervised learning"}]},{"type":"text","text":". Diagrams often label it \"unsupervised\" instead. We explain it fully in "},{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"module","key":"expert/advanced-llm","track":"engineers","lang":"en"},"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Advanced LLM"}]},{"type":"text","text":"."}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"why-we-dont-have-to-pre-train","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Why We don't Have to Pre-train?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"You do not need to pre-train your own model. Someone else already spent the millions. You can start from their finished model and "},{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"fine-tune"}]},{"type":"text","text":" it."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Fine-tuning"}]},{"type":"text","text":" means that you take a model that is already pre-trained, and you continue to train it. This time the dataset is small and task-specific, so the model gets good at one particular job."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"The clearest way to see the difference is to look at the data that each step eats:"}]},{"type":"image","number":"2.3","title":"Pre-training versus fine-tuning","note":"Pre-training takes a pile of unlabelled text, with no human in the loop. It produces general abilities, like completing and understanding text. Fine-tuning takes pairs that a human wrote: a prompt, and the response it should get. It adds specific abilities, like classification or question answering.","src":"course-images/fundamentals/pretrain-vs-finetune.webp","alt":"Pre-training versus fine-tuning","width":1280,"height":621},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"So, in one line each:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Pre-training"}]},{"type":"text","text":" eats "},{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"unlabelled"}]},{"type":"text","text":" text, collected from across the whole internet. Nobody writes the answers, because the next word "},{"type":"emphasis","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"is"}]},{"type":"text","text":" the answer."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Fine-tuning"}]},{"type":"text","text":" eats a "},{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"collection of pairs"}]},{"type":"text","text":": input and output, or prompt and response. People usually write or check these pairs. That is thousands of pairs, not billions of pages."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"That difference is why one costs millions and the other fits on a single GPU."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"fine-tuning","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Fine-tuning"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Imagine that you want a model that is very good at summarising articles."}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"items":[{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Start with a pre-trained base model."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Collect a dataset of examples: "},{"type":"code","text":"(long article, human-written short summary)"},{"type":"text","text":" pairs. A few thousand is often enough."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Continue to train the model on these pairs. This way, it learns the pattern \"long text in, short and accurate summary out\"."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"The result is clearly more consistent at summarising than the generic base model. And you do not pay for pre-training."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Here is what a few training rows can look like. This is exactly what the model sees, again and again, during step 3:"}]},{"type":"table","number":"2.1","title":"Fine-tuning","rowHeaders":false,"align":[null,null],"head":[{"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Article (input)"}]},{"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Human summary (output)"}]}],"rows":[[{"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"\"The city opened three new public parks this year, adding over 50 acres of green space. Officials say the parks will host weekend markets and free yoga classes starting next spring.\""}]},{"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"\"The city added 50 acres of new parks, which will host markets and yoga classes.\""}]}],[{"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"\"Scientists discovered a new species of frog in the Amazon rainforest. The frog has bright blue skin and is only 2 cm long, making it one of the smallest amphibians ever recorded.\""}]},{"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"\"A tiny, 2cm blue frog was discovered in the Amazon, one of the smallest amphibians on record.\""}]}],[{"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"\"The company's quarterly earnings report showed a 15% increase in revenue, driven mainly by strong sales in its cloud computing division.\""}]},{"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"\"The company's revenue grew 15% this quarter, led by cloud computing sales.\""}]}],[{"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"\"A new study found that drinking coffee in moderation may reduce the risk of heart disease. Researchers tracked 10,000 participants over five years.\""}]},{"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"\"A 5-year study of 10,000 people found moderate coffee drinking may lower heart disease risk.\""}]}],[{"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"\"The local football team won their championship game 3-1, securing their first title in a decade.\""}]},{"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"\"The team won the championship 3-1, their first title in ten years.\""}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"The model sees thousands of pairs like these, but it does not memorise the answers. It learns the "},{"type":"emphasis","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"pattern"}]},{"type":"text","text":": take the key facts out of a longer text, and compress them into one short, plain sentence."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"does-it-actually-work","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Does it Actually Work?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Let's test it on a new article that the model has never seen. It is not one of the articles in the table above."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Test article:"}]}]},{"type":"quote","blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"\"The tech startup announced today that it raised $50 million in Series B funding, led by two major venture capital firms. The company plans to use the funds to expand its engineering team and launch operations in three new countries by next year.\""}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Base model, before fine-tuning:"}]}]},{"type":"quote","blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"\"This is an interesting piece of news about a technology startup. The article talks about how the company managed to secure a significant amount of funding, specifically $50 million, in what is known as a Series B funding round, which was led by two prominent venture capital firms. According to the article, the startup intends to allocate this capital towards growing its engineering department and expanding its business operations into three additional countries within the coming year.\""}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Fine-tuned model, after:"}]}]},{"type":"quote","blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"\"The startup raised $50 million in Series B funding to grow its engineering team and expand into three new countries next year.\""}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Notice the difference. The answer of the base model is long and unfocused. It repeats the article almost sentence by sentence, and it opens with filler like \"This is an interesting piece of news\". The fine-tuned model learned the pattern from the table. Then it applied that pattern to an article it had never seen."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Here is a tiny, simplified example that uses Hugging Face "},{"type":"code","text":"transformers"},{"type":"text","text":":"}]},{"type":"code","language":"python","lines":[[{"text":"from","role":"keyword","bold":true},{"text":" transformers "},{"text":"import","role":"keyword","bold":true},{"text":" AutoModelForSeq2SeqLM, AutoTokenizer, Trainer, TrainingArguments"}],[],[{"text":"model "},{"text":"=","role":"keyword","bold":true},{"text":" AutoModelForSeq2SeqLM."},{"text":"from_pretrained(","role":"function"},{"text":"\"t5-small\"","role":"string"},{"text":")","role":"function"},{"text":"  # already pre-trained","role":"comment"}],[{"text":"tokenizer "},{"text":"=","role":"keyword","bold":true},{"text":" AutoTokenizer."},{"text":"from_pretrained(","role":"function"},{"text":"\"t5-small\"","role":"string"},{"text":")","role":"function"}],[],[{"text":"# dataset = a list of {\"article\": \"...\", \"summary\": \"...\"} pairs","role":"comment"}],[{"text":"trainer "},{"text":"=","role":"keyword","bold":true},{"text":" Trainer(","role":"function"}],[{"text":"    model","role":"function"},{"text":"=","role":"keyword","bold":true},{"text":"model,","role":"function"}],[{"text":"    args","role":"function"},{"text":"=","role":"keyword","bold":true},{"text":"TrainingArguments(output_dir","role":"function"},{"text":"=","role":"keyword","bold":true},{"text":"\"./summarizer\"","role":"string"},{"text":", num_train_epochs","role":"function"},{"text":"=","role":"keyword","bold":true},{"text":"3","role":"number"},{"text":"),","role":"function"}],[{"text":"    train_dataset","role":"function"},{"text":"=","role":"keyword","bold":true},{"text":"dataset,  ","role":"function"},{"text":"# your (article, summary) pairs","role":"comment"}],[{"text":")","role":"function"}],[{"text":"trainer."},{"text":"train()","role":"function"},{"text":"  # updates ALL of the model's parameters","role":"comment"}]]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"This is called "},{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"full fine-tuning"}]},{"type":"text","text":", because it updates every parameter. It is the same mechanism as pre-training, but on a much smaller dataset. It is cheaper than pre-training. But for a big model, it still needs a lot of GPU memory."}]},{"type":"diagram","number":"2.4","title":"Does it Actually Work?","svg":{"light":"api/v1/diagrams/faaa34e9648ab1f382b47772a3947d0f.svg","dark":"api/v1/diagrams/115a47dbd6ac28bfd34fb3665a87b0c6.svg"}},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"parameter-efficient-fine-tuning-peft","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Parameter-efficient Fine-tuning (PEFT)"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Full fine-tuning updates every parameter. For a big model, this still means large GPUs. Most of us do not have those."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"PEFT (Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning)"}]},{"type":"text","text":" solves this problem. It "},{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"freezes almost the whole model"}]},{"type":"text","text":", and it trains only a small number of new, extra parameters."}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"The frozen part stays exactly as pre-training left it."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Only a very small part of the parameters changes. This is often under 1% of the total."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"The most common PEFT technique is "},{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"LoRA"}]},{"type":"text","text":" (Low-Rank Adaptation). You do not need the maths. It is enough to know that LoRA is the cheap fine-tuning trick that almost everyone uses."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"This gives you three benefits. It runs on a single consumer GPU. It trains much faster. And it produces a small file that holds only the extra parameters, instead of a whole new copy of the model. This is what real projects use most of the time."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Here is a tiny, simplified LoRA example that uses Hugging Face "},{"type":"code","text":"peft"},{"type":"text","text":":"}]},{"type":"code","language":"python","lines":[[{"text":"from","role":"keyword","bold":true},{"text":" peft "},{"text":"import","role":"keyword","bold":true},{"text":" LoraConfig, get_peft_model"}],[{"text":"from","role":"keyword","bold":true},{"text":" transformers "},{"text":"import","role":"keyword","bold":true},{"text":" AutoModelForSeq2SeqLM"}],[],[{"text":"model "},{"text":"=","role":"keyword","bold":true},{"text":" AutoModelForSeq2SeqLM."},{"text":"from_pretrained(","role":"function"},{"text":"\"t5-small\"","role":"string"},{"text":")","role":"function"},{"text":"  # frozen base model","role":"comment"}],[],[{"text":"lora_config "},{"text":"=","role":"keyword","bold":true},{"text":" LoraConfig(r","role":"function"},{"text":"=","role":"keyword","bold":true},{"text":"8","role":"number"},{"text":", task_type","role":"function"},{"text":"=","role":"keyword","bold":true},{"text":"\"SEQ_2_SEQ_LM\"","role":"string"},{"text":")","role":"function"}],[{"text":"model "},{"text":"=","role":"keyword","bold":true},{"text":" get_peft_model(model, lora_config)","role":"function"},{"text":"  # freezes the base, adds small trainable layers","role":"comment"}],[],[{"text":"model."},{"text":"print_trainable_parameters()","role":"function"}],[{"text":"# something like: \"trainable params: 0.3M || all params: 60M || trainable%: 0.5%\"","role":"comment"}]]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Do not confuse PEFT with quantization, which "},{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"module","key":"fundamentals/llms","track":"engineers","lang":"en"},"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"LLM Fundamentals"}]},{"type":"text","text":" covered. "},{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"PEFT is about training cheaply. Quantization is about running cheaply."}]},{"type":"text","text":" People often use the two together."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"unsloth","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Unsloth"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"If you write the training loop yourself, you must connect a lot of parts by hand. Making a model fit in the memory that you have is a separate skill. "},{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"external","url":"https://unsloth.ai/docs"},"inlines":[{"type":"logo","src":"course-images/fundamentals/logos/unsloth.svg","alt":"","mono":false},{"type":"text","text":"Unsloth"}]},{"type":"text","text":" is an open-source library that does both of these jobs."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"What you get from it:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Fine-tuning that fits."}]},{"type":"text","text":" It rewrites the parts of the training code that use the most memory and time. Because of this, LoRA and quantized-LoRA runs need far less GPU memory, and they finish faster. Often, this decides whether a job can run on a free Colab GPU or not."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Ready-made notebooks."}]},{"type":"text","text":" The "},{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"external","url":"https://unsloth.ai/docs/get-started/unsloth-notebooks"},"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"notebook collection"}]},{"type":"text","text":" covers the popular open models. You open one, give it your dataset, and run it. This is the fastest honest way to go from \"I have some pairs\" to \"I have a fine-tuned model\"."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Pre-quantized models for running, not only for training."}]},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"module","key":"fundamentals/llms","track":"engineers","lang":"en"},"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"LLM Fundamentals"}]},{"type":"text","text":" mentioned that you rarely quantize anything yourself. These are the releases it meant. So the same project covers both jobs: compressing a model and training one."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"A guide, and a reality check."}]},{"type":"text","text":" The "},{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"external","url":"https://unsloth.ai/docs/get-started/fine-tuning-llms-guide"},"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"fine-tuning guide"}]},{"type":"text","text":" explains the whole process, step by step. Read the FAQ, "},{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"external","url":"https://unsloth.ai/docs/get-started/fine-tuning-for-beginners/faq-+-is-fine-tuning-right-for-me"},"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"is fine-tuning right for me"}]},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"before"}]},{"type":"text","text":" you start, because the honest answer is often no. Many problems look like fine-tuning problems. A better prompt, or the retrieval in "},{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"module","key":"fundamentals/rag","track":"engineers","lang":"en"},"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"RAG & Embeddings"}]},{"type":"text","text":", solves a lot of them."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"comparing-side-by-side","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Comparing Side-by-side"}]},{"type":"table","number":"2.2","title":"Comparing Side-by-side","rowHeaders":true,"align":[null,null,null,null],"head":[{"inlines":[]},{"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Pre-training"}]},{"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Full fine-tuning"}]},{"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"PEFT (e.g. LoRA)"}]}],"rows":[[{"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Starting point"}]},{"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Random weights"}]},{"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Pre-trained model"}]},{"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Pre-trained model"}]}],[{"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Data needed"}]},{"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"The whole internet, unlabelled"}]},{"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Task-specific pairs"}]},{"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Task-specific pairs"}]}],[{"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Parameters updated"}]},{"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"ALL, from scratch"}]},{"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"ALL"}]},{"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"SOME, often under 1%"}]}],[{"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Typical cost"}]},{"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Millions of dollars"}]},{"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Expensive, but far below pre-training"}]},{"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Cheap, fits on one GPU"}]}],[{"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Who does it"}]},{"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"A few big AI labs"}]},{"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Companies with real budgets"}]},{"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Most of us, most of the time"}]}]]},{"type":"diagram","number":"2.5","title":"Comparing Side-by-side","svg":{"light":"api/v1/diagrams/a67ee416bc4be23972269489a264c6c7.svg","dark":"api/v1/diagrams/d564128978e1994cc04e59201f714818.svg"}},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"when-not-to-fine-tune","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"When Not to Fine-tune"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Most people who think they need fine-tuning do not need it. So it is useful to know the three cases where the answer is no."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Do not fine-tune to teach a model your company's knowledge."}]},{"type":"text","text":" That knowledge is alive. The codebase gets new commits, contracts get updated, and policies change. Fine-tuning is a snapshot: it captures what your data said on one day, and it does not change after that. It takes hours and real money, so you cannot run it again every time a file changes. What you want there is retrieval, which is the topic of "},{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"module","key":"fundamentals/rag","track":"engineers","lang":"en"},"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"RAG & Embeddings"}]},{"type":"text","text":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Fine-tune for tasks, not for facts."}]},{"type":"text","text":" Fine-tuning is good at teaching a "},{"type":"emphasis","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"behaviour"}]},{"type":"text","text":", like summarising in a particular format or classifying text into your categories. It is poor at teaching "},{"type":"emphasis","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"facts"}]},{"type":"text","text":". Your few hundred pages land among billions of parameters, and those parameters hold everything else the model ever read."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"And remember who you are racing against."}]},{"type":"text","text":" Imagine that you spend a month fine-tuning a model for your task. By the time you finish, the next frontier model is out. A lab trained it with resources that you do not have. There is a good chance that this new model, with no changes, is better at your task than your fine-tuned version of the previous generation. Fine-tuning still wins when the task is genuinely yours and genuinely narrow. It loses when you try to beat the labs at training."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"what-is-hugging-face","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"What is Hugging Face?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Both code examples above import from it, so it is worth one paragraph."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"external","url":"https://huggingface.co/"},"inlines":[{"type":"logo","src":"course-images/fundamentals/logos/huggingface.svg","alt":"","mono":false},{"type":"text","text":"Hugging Face"}]},{"type":"text","text":" is the place where the open-source machine learning community keeps its work. Its "},{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Models"}]},{"type":"text","text":" page is, in practice, the central registry for open models of every kind, not only LLMs. It has models for computer vision, speech and audio, and many other areas. When a research group releases an open model, this is usually where it appears."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Hugging Face also publishes the libraries that everyone uses to work with those models. The main one is "},{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"code","text":"transformers"}]},{"type":"text","text":". It loads a model by name and runs it in a few lines of code. There are also companion libraries for the parts around it, such as "},{"type":"code","text":"peft"},{"type":"text","text":" for the LoRA training above, and "},{"type":"code","text":"datasets"},{"type":"text","text":" for the data that you give the model."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"key-terms","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Key Terms"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Parameters"}]},{"type":"text","text":": the numbers inside the network, also called weights."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Training"}]},{"type":"text","text":": changing the parameters by small amounts, using text, until the guesses are right."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Pre-training"}]},{"type":"text","text":": the largest step, which updates all parameters on unlabelled internet text and, for a large model, costs millions of dollars."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Fine-tuning"}]},{"type":"text","text":": continued training of a pre-trained model, on a small, task-specific set of input and output pairs."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Base model"}]},{"type":"text","text":": the general-purpose, pre-trained model that fine-tuning starts from."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Full fine-tuning"}]},{"type":"text","text":": fine-tuning that updates every parameter, which still needs a lot of GPU memory for a large model."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"PEFT"}]},{"type":"text","text":": fine-tuning that freezes almost the whole model and trains a few new parameters, often under 1%."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"LoRA"}]},{"type":"text","text":": Low-Rank Adaptation, the most common PEFT technique."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Unsloth"}]},{"type":"text","text":": an open-source library that cuts the memory and time of fine-tuning and also ships ready-made notebooks and pre-quantized models."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Hugging Face"}]},{"type":"text","text":": the central registry of open models, and the publisher of the libraries that load them."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"transformers"}]},{"type":"text","text":": the Hugging Face library that loads a model by name and runs it in a few lines of code."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"summary","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Summary"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Training is just the adjusting of a model's parameters. Pre-training starts from random weights, uses unlabelled internet text, and costs millions of dollars. Fine-tuning starts from someone else's finished model, uses pairs that a human wrote, and costs very little. PEFT does the same job but changes under 1% of the parameters. That is why most projects use it."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"But even a fine-tuned model still knows nothing about your private codebase or about today's data. RAG fills exactly that gap, and it is the next module."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"references","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"References"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"-articles","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"📄 Articles"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"external","url":"https://unsloth.ai/docs/get-started/fine-tuning-for-beginners/faq-+-is-fine-tuning-right-for-me"},"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Unsloth: is fine-tuning right for me?"}]},{"type":"text","text":": read this before you fine-tune anything"}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"external","url":"https://unsloth.ai/docs/get-started/unsloth-notebooks"},"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Unsloth notebooks"}]},{"type":"text","text":": ready-made notebooks for training and for running quantized models"}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"external","url":"https://unsloth.ai/docs/get-started/fine-tuning-llms-guide"},"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Unsloth fine-tuning guide"}]},{"type":"text","text":": the whole process, from start to finish"}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"external","url":"https://cloud.google.com/use-cases/fine-tuning-ai-models?hl=en#fine-tuning-llms-and-ai-models"},"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Fine-tuning AI models"}]},{"type":"text","text":": Google Cloud's overview, useful for the vocabulary"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"-videos","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"🎬 Videos"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"external","url":"https://youtube.com/shorts/Ei4E2lWStqw?si=q0PttlRmBsARGHZU"},"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"What is LLM Fine-Tuning? (Explained Simply)"}]},{"type":"text","text":": a short video, if you want the whole idea in under a minute"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"️-tools","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"🛠️ Tools"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"external","url":"https://unsloth.ai/docs"},"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Unsloth documentation"}]},{"type":"text","text":": the full documentation of the library"}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"external","url":"https://huggingface.co/"},"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Hugging Face"}]},{"type":"text","text":": the model registry, and the "},{"type":"code","text":"transformers"},{"type":"text","text":" library"}]}]}]}],"draft":null,"quiz":[{"question":"What does training change inside a model?","options":["It adds rules and facts that you can read.","It changes the size and shape of the network.","It adjusts the numbers inside the parameters.","It saves a copy of every text that it reads."],"answer":2,"explain":"Training only adjusts numbers, and the knowledge is spread across billions of them. The shape of the network does not change."},{"question":"How is the data for pre-training different from the data for fine-tuning?","options":["Pre-training uses unlabelled text, and fine-tuning uses input and output pairs.","Pre-training uses input and output pairs, and fine-tuning uses unlabelled text.","Both steps use unlabelled text, but fine-tuning uses a smaller amount of it.","Both steps use pairs that people wrote, but pre-training uses many more of them."],"answer":0,"explain":"In pre-training, the next word is the answer, so nobody writes labels. Fine-tuning uses thousands of pairs that people wrote or checked."},{"question":"What does PEFT, for example LoRA, do during fine-tuning?","options":["It updates all parameters, but on a smaller dataset.","It compresses the model so that it runs more cheaply.","It starts from random weights, but it uses less data.","It freezes most of the model and trains a few extra parameters."],"answer":3,"explain":"PEFT freezes almost the whole model and trains a small number of new parameters, often under 1%. Quantization is the different idea that makes a model cheaper to run."},{"question":"Your company wants a model that knows its codebase and its policies. Both change often. What should you use?","options":["Fine-tune the model again each time a file changes.","Use retrieval (RAG), and do not fine-tune for this.","Pre-train a new model on the company's own data.","Use PEFT on the documents, because PEFT is cheap."],"answer":1,"explain":"Fine-tuning is a snapshot, and it teaches a behaviour well but facts poorly. For knowledge that changes often, use retrieval with RAG."}]}